  <!-- LOCAL KNOWLEDGE SECTION -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="pf-section-label">MAGHULL LOCAL KNOWLEDGE</span>
      <h2>How L31 Plumbing Actually Looks</h2>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <p>Maghull is in Sefton Metropolitan Borough Council, not Liverpool. Molyneux ward, Park ward, Sudell ward. Population around 28,000. A dormitory town for Liverpool, with strong rail links to the city centre, Ormskirk and beyond.</p>

        <p>It's almost entirely a 20th-century town. The railway came in 1849. The Northway A59 was built in 1933 as one of Britain's first dual carriageways. Most of the housing went up between the 1930s and the 1980s. The population doubled in the 1950s and again in the 1970s.</p>

        <p>The old village core is at Red Lion Bridge, where the original ridge of high ground gave protection from the River Alt flooding. The Leeds and Liverpool Canal runs through. The Maghull Homes Manor House from 1888 still stands.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        <p>Frank Hornby, inventor of Hornby trains, Meccano and Dinky Toys, lived in Maghull. His house in the grounds of Maricourt Catholic High School was the first building outside London to receive a Blue Plaque. That's the kind of place Maghull is.</p>

        <p>The housing tells the story of 20th-century English suburbia. 1930s bay-window semis. 1950s post-war planned estates. 1970s dormitory cul-de-sacs. 1990s and 2000s infill. 2010s Sefton East new builds. Plus McCarthy and Stone retirement flats on the Royal British Legion site. Each era has its own plumbing patterns.</p>

        <p>We've worked them all. Same fixed rates whichever decade your home is from.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- COMMON JOBS -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f1f5f9;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Common Emergency Jobs in Maghull</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">What we attend most often in L31.</p>
      <div class="jobs-grid" style="margin-top:2rem;">

        <div class="job-card">
          <h3>System Boiler Failures</h3>
          <div class="job-meta"><span class="job-price">Gas Safe registered</span><span class="job-time">15 min response</span></div>
          <p>Most Maghull homes have system boilers feeding a hot water cylinder. Pump seal failures, three-port valve sticking and heat exchanger corrosion are the usual culprits. We carry parts for the common brands.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="job-card">
          <h3>Hot Water Cylinder Faults</h3>
          <div class="job-meta"><span class="job-price">Gas Safe registered</span><span class="job-time">15 min response</span></div>
          <p>A leaking, scaled or failed cylinder leaves you with no hot water. Immersion heater replacement from £150 same day. Full cylinder swap from £600. Unvented work needs G3 qualification, which our engineers hold.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="job-card">
          <h3>Commuter Evening Call-Outs</h3>
          <div class="job-meta"><span class="job-price">Gas Safe registered</span><span class="job-time">15 min response</span></div>
          <p>Most Maghull plumbing faults are discovered at 6pm or 7pm when you get home from work. We treat evening calls the same as any other. Weekday rates apply until 9pm. We don't try to push you into an out-of-hours rate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="job-card">
          <h3>1960s Copper Pipe Pinholes</h3>
          <div class="job-meta"><span class="job-price">Gas Safe registered</span><span class="job-time">15 min response</span></div>
          <p>The 1950s and 1960s estates have original copper pipework now 60 to 70 years old. Pinhole leaks under floors and in lofts are common. We patch the failing section and check the rest of the run for early signs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="job-card">
          <h3>Blocked Drains</h3>
          <div class="job-meta"><span class="job-price">Gas Safe registered</span><span class="job-time">15 min response</span></div>
          <p>The older clay drain runs are at the end of their life. Tree roots find their way in. Ground movement cracks pipes. We clear with high-pressure jetting. CCTV survey on request to find the source.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="job-card">
          <h3>Retirement Flat Block Emergencies</h3>
          <div class="job-meta"><span class="job-price">Gas Safe registered</span><span class="job-time">15 min response</span></div>
          <p>McCarthy and Stone and other retirement schemes have shared boiler systems and communal hot water. A fault affects every flat. We coordinate with managing agents and provide written reports.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- WHAT TO DO SECTION -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>While You Wait For Our Engineer</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">Four steps to protect your home while we're on the way.</p>
      <div class="pf-who-grid" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes"><span class="pf-who-icon">1</span><div><strong>Find the stopcock and turn it off</strong><p>For any water leak, the stopcock is your first move. In most Maghull semis it's under the kitchen sink, in the garage, or near the front door. In newer estates it's often in the airing cupboard. Turn it clockwise to stop all water.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes"><span class="pf-who-icon">2</span><div><strong>If you smell gas, get out</strong><p>Don't touch any switches. Open windows as you leave. Call <a href="https://cadentgas.com/emergencies" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--orange);font-weight:700;">Cadent Gas on 0800 111 999</a> first. Then us on 0151 558 0334 once the home is safe.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes"><span class="pf-who-icon">3</span><div><strong>No heating? Check the pressure gauge</strong><p>Look at the gauge on the boiler. It should sit between 1 and 1.5 bar. Below 0.5, you can top it up with the filling loop underneath. That fixes about half of all "no heating" calls. If it doesn't, call us.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes"><span class="pf-who-icon">4</span><div><strong>Photograph the damage</strong><p>Take photos and video before you start cleaning. Insurance claims need proof. We give you our own fault report with photos as well, ready for your insurer.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


